        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 862,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 776,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Makes an array of any shape with every element set to the same value.\n\nnp.zeros and np.ones do this for 0 and 1; np.full does it for anything else - a starting level of 0.5 for every channel, -1 to mark \"no reading yet\", a background value for an image, a default weight for every joint.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.full <dims ...> <value>\nDefault: 3 values of 0. With one number it is the length; with two or more, the last\nis the value and the rest are the shape.\n\nEXAMPLES:\nnp.full 10 0.5\nnp.full 4 4 -1\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\n(button):\nMakes the array and sends it.\n\nshape (text box, also an inlet):\nThe size along each axis: 10 for a list of 10, 2 3 for 2 rows of 3.\n\nvalue (float box, also an inlet):\nThe value every element gets.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nfull out:\nThe array (32-bit floats).\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nMade at load: 2 rows of 3, all 7; and a list of 5, all 0.5. Change a shape or a value and click its button.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.zeros and np.ones fill with 0 and 1. t.full is the PyTorch version. np.tile repeats a whole array rather than one value.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Makes an array of any shape with every element set to the same value.\n\nnp.zeros and np.ones do this for 0 and 1; np.full does it for anything else - a starting level of 0.5 for every channel, -1 to mark \"no reading yet\", a background value for an image, a default weight for every joint.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.full <dims ...> <value>\nDefault: 3 values of 0. With one number it is the length; with two or more, the last\nis the value and the rest are the shape.\n\nEXAMPLES:\nnp.full 10 0.5\nnp.full 4 4 -1\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\n(button):\nMakes the array and sends it.\n\nshape (text box, also an inlet):\nThe size along each axis: 10 for a list of 10, 2 3 for 2 rows of 3.\n\nvalue (float box, also an inlet):\nThe value every element gets.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nfull out:\nThe array (32-bit floats).\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nMade at load: 2 rows of 3, all 7; and a list of 5, all 0.5. Change a shape or a value and click its button.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.zeros and np.ones fill with 0 and 1. t.full is the PyTorch version. np.tile repeats a whole array rather than one value."
        },
